WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.590
Everything is science.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:05.630
So there’s not anything you can touch or
see or do that has nothing to do with science.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.090
Many students can tell you that I get really
carried away sometimes.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.550
I get so carried away and so excited about
what I’m teaching.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.180
Yeah, actually so that’s nine.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:18.960
And I think that’s infectious for them as
well and it makes them really excited and

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:19.500
want to learn.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.099
I tell them right away, “You will struggle
in this class.”

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:27.099
And that’s okay.

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:30.130
I struggled in class, in different classes,
along the way.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.810
And that’s the only way that we’re going
to learn something.

00:00:34.929 --> 00:00:38.809
The science classes that I taught, probably
in my first 10 years, students were not very

00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:40.040
active in doing science.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.400
There was very little ability for students
to fail at any of the activities we did.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.800
So I had this conception when I started teaching
that in 10 years I was going to be a master

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:52.800
teacher.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.070
It took me really up until this year to recognize
that perfection is a myth.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.489
What scientific concept are we looking at
Mylani?

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:02.489
Friction.

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:03.510
What’s friction?

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:09.590
Without that initial engagement or really
enjoyment of learning, they’re not going

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.780
to want to pursue science at later ages.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:16.680
I hope that they look at the world in a different
way and see science.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.500
Also guys.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.500
Gloves.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.590
Glove up.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.549
The opportunities of science come from the
unexpected that come through, which include

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:26.549
non-results.

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:30.240
Because the process of science is fraught
with running protocols and not having them

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.740
work but then having interesting questions
that arise from that process.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.930
It’s not just about a struggle problem.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:40.990
It’s about struggling and getting through
and learning through your actual struggle,

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:41.990
like persevering.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.840
I want them to be able to stand on their own
two feet and prove to somebody else whether

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.829
in school or in life why something is correct
or why something makes sense to them.

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:56.700
With a little bit more time to explore other
dimensions of physics, I can construct these

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.079
units and these projects that are more authentic.

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:06.280
And so they see what they could do if they
were to pursue an engineering career or pursue

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.500
something in computer science, as opposed
to just pure applied physics.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.000
Regardless of whether we’ve been teaching
five, 10, 20 years, there’s always some

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.550
other way that we can grow and develop.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.710
Something else that we can learn and potentially
share with our students.

